\begin_body

\begin_layout Standard
In the 2015 NIST loophole-free EPRB/Bell experiment (Shalm et al.) the “client” at A or B is essentially a fast,
 randomly switched polarization analyser followed by a single-photon detector and time tagger.
 The Pockels cell is what rapidly changes the measurement basis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
One measurement station
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The optical path at either A or B can be pictured approximately as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boxed{\text{incoming photon}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{wave plates}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{Pockels cell}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{polarizer}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{SNSPD}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{time tagger}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The actual NIST receiver uses two half-wave plates,
 a quarter-wave plate,
 a Pockels cell and two plate polarizers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The important point is that the Pockels cell is not the photon detector.
 It is an electro-optical polarization rotator.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
A random bit chooses the measurement
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
While the photon is travelling from the source toward A,
 A's random-number system generates a bit,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x=0\quad\text{or}\quad x=1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
B independently generates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=0\quad\text{or}\quad y=1
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
These select between the two Bell-test measurement settings:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x=0\rightarrow a,\qquad x=1\rightarrow a'
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=0\rightarrow b,\qquad y=1\rightarrow b'
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST actually XORed outputs from two random-number generators together with predetermined pseudorandom bits.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The random bit controls the Pockels cell
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is the clever part.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For random bit 0 the Pockels cell is left OFF.
 The incoming polarization therefore encounters the analyser in one effective orientation:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{PC OFF}\quad\Rightarrow\quad a
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
for A or 
\begin_inset Formula $b$
\end_inset

 for B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For random bit 1 a high voltage is rapidly applied:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{PC ON}\quad\Rightarrow\quad a'
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
or 
\begin_inset Formula $b'$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The electric field changes the birefringence of the Pockels-cell crystal.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consequently the photon's polarization is rotated before it encounters the fixed polarizer.
 Thus the experiment doesn't mechanically rotate a polarizer between 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a'$
\end_inset

;
 it electrically rotates the polarization relative to a fixed analyser.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Conceptually,
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
|\psi\rangle\xrightarrow{\quad U_{{\rm PC}}(x)\quad}|\psi_{x}\rangle\xrightarrow{\quad\text{polarizer}\quad}\text{detector}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The polarizer performs the projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
After the Pockels cell comes a very high-contrast polarization analyser.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The two NIST plate polarizers together have a contrast ratio greater than 
\begin_inset Formula $7000:1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is where an important subtlety arises.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This particular NIST experiment does not implement the measurement like a standard EPRB analyser:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\begin{array}{ccc}
 &  & D_{+}\\
\text{photon} & \rightarrow & \text{PBS}\\
 &  & D_{-}
\end{array}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
with two detectors,
 one representing 
\begin_inset Formula $+1$
\end_inset

 and one representing 
\begin_inset Formula $-1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The NIST setup uses the polarizer as a transmit/reject analyser and the transmitted photon is coupled back into single-mode fibre and sent to one superconducting nanowire single-photon detector (SNSPD) at each station.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So at A the primitive result is essentially
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
A=\begin{cases}
1 & \text{photon detected in the measurement window}\\
0 & \text{no photon detected}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
rather than a direct 
\begin_inset Formula $+1/-1$
\end_inset

 result from two output detectors.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
That distinction is important for interpreting the NIST raw data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The SNSPD produces the detection event
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A photon that passes the analyser travels through a single-mode fibre to a superconducting nanowire detector which converts the absorption of a photon into an electrical pulse.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The NIST paper reports detector efficiencies of 
\begin_inset Formula $91\pm2\%$
\end_inset

 and total system efficiencies of approximately:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\eta_{A}=74.7\%,\qquad\eta_{B}=75.6\%.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Those high efficiencies are crucial because the experiment is designed to close the detection/fair-sampling loophole.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The time tagger records the result
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The electrical pulse from the SNSPD is amplified and sent to a time tagger:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{SNSPD pulse}\rightarrow\text{amplifier}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{time tag}}
\]

\end_inset

so the recorded data are fundamentally event times associated with the measurement settings rather than simply a pre-made table of 
\begin_inset Formula $A=\pm1,B=\pm1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
NIST explicitly says that the measurement outcome is taken to be fixed when it is recorded by the time tagger.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is why the NIST repository contains large separate A and B event files—
the raw experimental output consists of the locally recorded streams.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Putting the whole operation together
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For A:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boxed{\text{RNG}}\longrightarrow x\longrightarrow\boxed{\text{Pockels-cell voltage}}
\]

\end_inset

while simultaneously
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boxed{\text{photon}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{PC}(x)}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{polarizer}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{SNSPD}}\rightarrow\boxed{t_{A}}.
\]

\end_inset

B independently performs
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boxed{\text{RNG}}\longrightarrow y\longrightarrow\boxed{\text{PC}(y)}
\]

\end_inset

and
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\boxed{\text{photon}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{PC}(y)}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{polarizer}}\rightarrow\boxed{\text{SNSPD}}\rightarrow\boxed{t_{B}}.
\]

\end_inset

T
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In the NIST experiment A and B's detectors were 184 m apart with the source 126 m and 132 m from them.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The random setting are selected while the photons are in flight.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This also explains something important about the data analysis we were doing previously:
 we should not automatically interpret the NIST detector records as conventional two-channel 
\begin_inset Formula $+1/-1$
\end_inset

 polarizer outcomes.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The physical measurement is a randomly selected projection followed by a detection/no-detection result.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
How that gets converted into the 
\begin_inset Formula $A,B$
\end_inset

 variables used in a particular processed file therefore matters considerably.
\end_layout

